==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₃er-}}
From {{bor|en|la|abortiō|abortiōnem|t=miscarriage, abortion}}, from {{m|la|aborior|t=to miscarry}}. Equivalent to {{suffix|en|abort|ion}}. Displaced native {{ncog|ang|ǣwyrp|lit=throwing out, rejection}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|əʹbôrshən|a=US}}, {{IPA|en|/əˈbɔɹ.ʃn̩/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-abortion.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɔː(ɹ)ʃən|s=3}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{senseid|en|Q8452}} {{lb|en|medicine}} The expulsion from the [[womb]] of a [[fetus]] or [[embryo]] before it is fully developed, with loss of the fetus.{{defdate|from 16th c.}}
## {{lb|en|now uncommon in general use}} A [[spontaneous abortion]]; a [[miscarriage]].
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1605|author=[[w:Josuah Sylvester|Josuah, transl. Sylvester]]|chapter=The Third Day of the First Week|title=Devine Weekes and Workes|original=La Premiere Sepmaine|by=w:Guillaume de Salluste Du Bartas|origyear=1578|lines=693–696|passage=Swines-bread, so used, doth not onely speed / A tardy labour; but (without great heed) / If over it a Child-great Woman stride, / Instant '''abortion''' often doth betide.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1809|author=William Nicholson|title=The British Encyclopaedia|volume=IV
|passage=At any time after impregnation, '''abortion''' may take place: it is one of the most common complaints of pregnancy, whence it is a matter of no small consequence that every practitioner should well understand it.}}
## An [[induced abortion]].
##: {{ux|en|Mary decided to have an '''abortion''' because she was too young to raise a baby.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1997|author=w:George Carlin|title=w:Brain Droppings|url=https://archive.org/details/isbn_9780786883219/|location=[[New York]]|publisher=w:Hyperion Books|ISBN=0-7868-8321-9|LCCN=96-52373|OCLC=36084460|OL=26335012M|page=93|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/isbn_9780786883219/page/93/|text=It is impossible for an '''abortion''' clinic to have a waiting list of more than nine months.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=20 Jan 2014|author=Didi Kirsten Tatlow|title=‘She. Herself. Naked.': The Art of {{w|He Chengyao}}|journal=w:The New York Times|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230816082146/https://archive.nytimes.com/sinosphere.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/20/she-herself-naked-the-art-of-he-chengyao/|archivedate=16 August 2023|url=https://archive.nytimes.com/sinosphere.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/20/she-herself-naked-the-art-of-he-chengyao/|issn=0362-4331|oclc=1645522|section=Sinosphere|url2=https://archive.li/MEQFH|text=The story of Ms. He and her mother began in the early 1960s, shortly before the Cultural Revolution shook China. Her young parents, who worked in a pottery factory in Rongchang in present-day Chongqing municipality, conceived her while unmarried. “They were told by the factory, ‘Have an '''abortion''' or be fired’,” she said. They chose to keep her and were fired.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=5 October 2017|author=Ben Jacobs|journal=The Guardian|url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2017/oct/05/tim-murphy-republican-congress-abortion|passage=Representative Tim Murphy of Pennsylvania will resign from Congress after claims that the anti-abortion Republican had urged a woman he was having an extramarital affair with to have an '''abortion'''.}}
# {{lb|en|now|rare}} An aborted fetus; an [[abortus]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1791|author=w:James Boswell|title=Life of Johnson|page=657|publisher=Oxford|year_published=2008
|passage=‘It seems too hairy for an '''abortion''', and too small for a mature birth.’}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1929|author=w:Virginia Woolf|title=A Room of One's Own
|passage=The Fascist poem, one may fear, will be a horrid little '''abortion''' such as one sees in a glass jar in the museum of some county town.}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} A misshapen person or thing; a [[monstrosity]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1846|author=w:Charles Dickens|title=Pictures from Italy|url=http://etext.virginia.edu/etcbin/ot2www-pubeng?specfile=/texts/english/modeng/publicsearch/modengpub.o2w&act=text&offset=269656481&textreg=2&query=abortion&id=DicPict|chapter=10|passage=Insomuch that I do honestly believe, there can be no place in the world, where such intolerable '''abortions''', begotten of the sculptor’s chisel, are to be found in such profusion, as in Rome.}}
#* {{quote-journal|1=en|year=1889|journal= Harper's New Monthly Magazine|location=New York|author=w:Edward Bellamy|title=To Whom This May Come|page=459|column=2|text=His voice was the most pitiable '''abortion''' of a voice I had ever heard.}}
#* {{quote-newsgroup|en|year=2000|author=Jules|title=please dont buy beacon cd|newsgroup=alt.fan.allman-brothers|passage=Dickey on his own manages to turn a simple bo diddley 1-2-3-4 into an absolute '''abortion''' of a song.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2003|author=David Kerekes|title=Headpress 24: Powered by Love|page=133|passage=an absolute '''abortion''' of a book}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} Failure or [[abandonment]] of a project, promise, goal etc. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=1800-09-23
|title=Letter to Dr. Benjamin Rush
|author=w:Thomas Jefferson
|url=http://memory.loc.gov/cgi-bin/query/r?ammem/mtj:@field(DOCID+@lit(tj090069))
|passage=The returning good sense of our country threatens '''abortion''' to their hopes, & they believe that any portion of power confided to me, will be exerted in opposition to their schemes.
}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2013|author=Fakhry A. Assaad; James W. LaMoreaux; Travis Hughes|title=Field Methods for Geologists and Hydrogeologists|isbn=3662054388|page=314|passage=The transfer or loss of the project manager before the project is completed will result in lost continuity and delay or the '''abortion''' of the project and/or the report.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2015|author=Gabriele Brandstetter|title=Poetics of Dance: Body, Image, and Space|isbn=0190266864|page=73|passage={{...}} the abrupt '''abortion''' of the trip after eleven days.}}
# {{lb|en|biology}} Arrest of development of any organ, so that it remains an [[imperfect]] formation or is [[absorbed]]. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
# The cessation of an illness or disease at a very early stage.

